Our distributed cache shows inconsistent reads after a rolling deploy — some nodes serve stale data for up to 10 minutes, and support has escalated three tickets from customers seeing their own just-saved changes disappear and reappear depending on which pod handles the request.

Cache invalidation code:
```js
function invalidate(key) {
  redis.del(key);
  localCache.delete(key); // per-process in-memory cache, TTL 10 minutes, read-through on miss
}
function onDataChanged(key, newValue) {
  db.save(key, newValue);
  invalidate(key);
}
function read(key) {
  if (localCache.has(key)) return localCache.get(key);
  const value = redis.get(key) ?? db.load(key);
  localCache.set(key, value);
  return value;
}
```

Deployment config: rolling deploy, old and new pods coexist for up to 5 minutes during rollout, 6 pods total behind a load balancer with no session affinity, each pod with its own in-process `localCache` that nothing else in the fleet can reach.

Incident timeline, reconstructed from logs: a write happens on pod A (already on the new version) which calls `invalidate(key)`, correctly clearing Redis and pod A's own local cache; the very next request for that key, routed by the load balancer to pod D, misses pod D's local cache (good), reads through to Redis, gets the fresh value, and correctly caches it locally on pod D — but pods B, C, E, and F, which had already cached the old value on an earlier read before the write, are never notified of the invalidation at all and keep serving their own stale `localCache` entries until each pod's individual 10-minute TTL happens to expire on its own clock.

What's the root cause and what would actually fix it, given we can't just set the TTL to zero (that was tried in a hotfix and tanked Redis QPS by roughly 40x), and given we'd like a solution that doesn't require every pod to poll Redis on a tight interval just to stay in sync?

This Rust service occasionally deadlocks under load, but only in production with real traffic patterns; we can't reproduce it in staging even with a synthetic load generator that mimics the request mix as closely as we can tell.

Relevant locking code, three methods on the same struct:
```rust
struct Cache {
    data: Mutex<HashMap<String, Vec<u8>>>,
    stats: Mutex<Stats>,
}
impl Cache {
    fn get_or_compute(&self, key: &str, compute: impl Fn() -> Vec<u8>) -> Vec<u8> {
        let mut data = self.data.lock().unwrap();
        if let Some(v) = data.get(key) {
            self.record_hit();
            return v.clone();
        }
        let value = compute();
        data.insert(key.to_string(), value.clone());
        self.record_miss();
        value
    }
    fn record_hit(&self) {
        let mut stats = self.stats.lock().unwrap();
        stats.hits += 1;
    }
    fn record_miss(&self) {
        let mut stats = self.stats.lock().unwrap();
        stats.misses += 1;
    }
    // added two weeks ago for a metrics endpoint, called every 10s by a background task
    fn report(&self) -> String {
        let stats = self.stats.lock().unwrap();
        let data = self.data.lock().unwrap();
        format!("hits={} misses={} size={}", stats.hits, stats.misses, data.len())
    }
}
```

Thread dump from the incident shows two threads: one blocked acquiring `stats` while holding `data` (inside `get_or_compute` calling into `record_hit`/`record_miss`), another blocked acquiring `data` while holding `stats` (inside `report`).

Our current, wrong hypothesis — which we spent a day chasing before giving up — was that `compute()` itself was reentrant and calling back into `get_or_compute`. It isn't; `compute` is a pure, non-reentrant closure in every call site we've checked, and none of them touch the cache. `report()` was added two weeks ago for a metrics endpoint and shipped without anyone connecting it to this code path during review. Given the thread dump, what's actually going on, and what's the fix, keeping in mind `report()` needs a consistent snapshot of both stats and size?

